Useful scripts
- `src/data_generator.py` — generates realistic synthetic sales data (~10k–50k records).
- `src/data_cleaning.py` — cleans and validates the raw dataset.
- `src/load_to_postgres.py` — loads cleaned data into PostgreSQL (requires `.env` with credentials).
- `src/feature_engineering.py` — prepares customer- and time-level features.
- `src/customer_segmentation.py` — K-Means segmentation and cluster summaries.
- `src/sales_forecasting.py` — monthly forecasting with naive, seasonal-naive, Linear Regression, and Random Forest comparisons.
- `src/eda.py` — business-focused figures and summary outputs.

Outputs
- `outputs/figures/` — monthly trends, category/region comparisons, discount analysis, and customer distribution.
- `outputs/insights/` — KPI, category, region, cluster, and forecast summaries.
- `outputs/predictions/` — actual versus predicted monthly sales.
- `models/` — K-Means and forecasting model artifacts.

The generated data, outputs, models, local environment, and secrets are
ignored by Git. They can be recreated with `python scripts/run_pipeline.py`.

## Dataset sources and importing external retail data

The default source is the fictional synthetic RetailX dataset. The dashboard
also supports an explicit CSV upload workflow with column mapping, strict
parsing, duplicate detection, return preservation, and an import validation
report before activation. Imported datasets are labelled with their selected
provenance. Profit and margin are shown as unavailable when the source does
not provide cost/profit data.

The public-data audit covers the [UCI Online Retail dataset](https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/352/online-retail),
which contains 541,909 UK online-retail transaction rows from 2010-12-01 to
2011-12-09 and is licensed CC BY 4.0. UCI identifies `InvoiceNo`, `StockCode`,
`Description`, `Quantity`, `InvoiceDate`, `UnitPrice`, `CustomerID`, and
`Country`; cancellation invoices and negative quantities must remain visible
in the import report. See [DATASETS.md](docs/DATASETS.md) and
[data_import.py](src/data_import.py) for the policy and implementation.

The import workflow is deliberately source-agnostic: review the suggested
mapping in the dashboard, select the five required fields, optionally set a
delimiter and date format, validate the report, and then activate the data.
Accepted data is stored in an isolated local workspace under `data/workspaces`
and can be exported as a filtered CSV. This local adapter is single-user
development storage; it does not claim PostgreSQL-grade multi-user controls.
The optional XLSX path uses `openpyxl` when installed from
`requirements-optional.txt`.

See [ARCHITECTURE.md](docs/ARCHITECTURE.md), [SECURITY.md](docs/SECURITY.md),
and [VERIFICATION.md](docs/VERIFICATION.md) for module boundaries and verified
limitations.

## Streamlit application

`app.py` provides an Executive Overview, Sales Analysis, Customer Analytics, Forecasting, and
Data Quality page. It reads the cleaned CSV for a self-contained local demo;
PostgreSQL remains available through `src/load_to_postgres.py` for database
integration. Forecasting compares naive, seasonal-naive, Linear Regression,
and Random Forest models using expanding-window validation followed by one
untouched chronological holdout. Lagged models forecast recursively so actual
test-period sales are not used as future features. Metrics include MAE, RMSE,
and R²; results are a baseline on synthetic data and must not be treated as a
guarantee.

## Validation and optional tracking

`src/validation.py` checks required fields, nulls, dates, numeric ranges,
duplicates, and financial consistency. Core tests are in `tests/` and do not
require PostgreSQL credentials. Forecast tests cover chronological splitting,
target/horizon alignment, leakage prevention, baselines, metrics, insufficient
history, finite output, and reproducibility. MLflow is optional; install it
from `requirements-optional.txt` when experiment tracking is needed.

## Verified forecasting result

The current synthetic dataset has 48 calendar months and produces 46 usable
monthly feature rows after the two lag warm-up rows. The final evaluation is a
12-month holdout (January–December 2023) after a 34-month training period.
The model selected by three expanding-window validation folds of three months
is the `Naive` baseline. On the final holdout, the selected baseline has
MAE $25,378.70, RMSE $28,195.25, and R² -4.2684. The negative R² is retained:
it means this forecast is worse than predicting the holdout mean under that
metric, and it is evidence that this synthetic series is not yet predictable
enough for a production claim. Final holdout comparisons are written to
`outputs/insights/forecast_metrics.csv`.

Power BI
The data model, DAX measures, page layout, relationships, and visual recommendations are documented in `powerbi/dax_measures.md` and `powerbi/POWERBI_BUILD_GUIDE.md`. Power BI Desktop is required to save the final `.pbix` report.

